  "final_message": "I wrote `answer.json` with 13 functions that can return `None` as a normal result:\n\n- `billing.api._optional_str`: returns `None` when the key is missing or null.\n- `billing.api._match`: returns `None` when the route pattern doesn't match.\n- `billing.catalog.find_price`: returns `None` when no active price matches.\n- `billing.customers.default_payment_method`: returns `None` when the customer has no default method.\n- `billing.engine.BillingEngine._renew`: returns `None` for a canceled subscription with no metered items.\n- `billing.engine.BillingEngine.pay`: passes through `payments.collect`, so it returns `None` when nothing is charged.\n- `billing.payments.collect`: returns `None` when the invoice is already settled, has no payment method, or has an expired card.\n- `billing.store.Store.get_customer`, `get_price`, `get_subscription`, `get_invoice`, `get_coupon`: return `None` for an unknown ID.\n- `billing.tax.lookup`: returns `None` when no tax rate matches the region.\n\nI read every module in the package and grepped for `return None`, `| None` annotations and `.get(` calls to cross-check. I excluded functions annotated `-> None`, functions that raise on a missing key (such as the `require_*` methods and `fx.RateTable.rate`), and the `Protocol` stubs in `models.py`, which have no implementation. I did not run the test suite, since it would write `.pytest_cache` into the repo and you asked that no other files change.",
